# Branch flow guardrail

The flow is `develop -> staging -> release`, and both promotions are human
steps. `staging` and `release` are protected branches: `release` accepts
merges only from `staging`, `staging` only from `develop`. `main` is not part
of the flow and is never a target.

- Cut every working branch from `develop`. Open every PR against `develop`
  (`gh pr create --base develop`). Merge only PRs whose base is `develop`.
- Never push to, commit on, merge into, rebase onto, or open or merge a PR
  targeting `staging`, `release`, or `main`; never cut a branch from them;
  never merge or rebase them into a working branch. Read-only use (checkout
  to inspect, log, diff, fetch) is fine.
- An emergency exception (hotfix straight to staging or release) is a human
  decision executed by a human. Prepare the change on a branch from
  `develop` and hand the promotion over; do not do it yourself.

Full rule: `AGENTS.md`, "Branch flow guardrail". `scripts/agent-branch-flow-guard.sh`
runs before every shell command (chained from the ISO build guard) and blocks
the common violations; the rule, not the hook, is the constraint.

This guardrail covers the `ffos-user` and `ffos` repositories only (matched by repository name, any owner, any remote or URL of the clone). A session that also works in another repository, such as `ff-app` or `play`, whose only branch is `main`, follows that repository's own flow there. The guard lets those commands through only when it can positively tell they target another repository: an unquoted `gh -R/--repo` or `git -C <dir>`, or the shell's own working directory when the command contains no `cd`/`chdir`/`pushd`/`popd`. REST calls (`gh api`, `api.github.com`) are never let through this way. It never follows a `cd`, so do cross-repo work with `git -C` and `gh -R`. Anything it can't read keeps the full rules: an unresolvable target, escapes or multi-line quoted strings, a background `&`, a heredoc, `sh -c`/`eval`/`env`, any `GIT_*`/`GH_*` assignment, a `$(...)`/backtick running git or gh, `--git-dir`, or a `#` comment. So does a command that names a flow repository in a URL or GitHub path, PR URLs included. For cross-repo PRs and commits, pass text with `--body-file` / `git commit -F <file>` rather than a heredoc or `$(...)`.
